# B-plan experiment (owner-authorized flash): SLAVE with ACTIVE scan vs the laptop fake dongle.
# 2026-09-28 laptop side. Firmware esp32s3-rgb-slave-obdtest, one line changed:
#   lib/dashcore/obd_transport_ble.cpp  scan_->setActiveScan(false) -> (true)
# Fake dongle: obd-ble-sim.py --mode sweep --rpm 2400 --coolant 90 --intake 42 --speed 55
#
# RESULT: *** B PLAN SUCCEEDS *** -- the board connects, subscribes, and exchanges AT/PID traffic.
#   board side : obd-ble: 特征已配齐（FFF1 已订阅 / FFF2 可写）⇒ state=ready
#                link: 启动闸门开了 —— BLE 已连上(ready),等了 14759ms
#                obd-ble: state=ready peer=40:ab:3d:ef:3b:df conn=1 drop=0 connects=1
#                SRC speed=van rpm=obd coolant=obd intake=obd | ... 90.0C 42.0C   (= the sim's values)
#   link side  : link: locked tick_age=38ms seen=250 seq_gap=0 miss=0   (ESP-NOW unaffected)
#   sim side   : client subscribed (subscribed clients now 1)
#                cmd=186 notify=234 write=744 err=0
# => the slave's BLE central + ESP-NOW RX COEXIST fine. The active-scan change was the unlock.
#
# One observation worth chasing later (NOT the subject of this test): the AT requests arriving at
# the simulator look byte-serialized (`0`,`01`,`C`,`\r`) with interleaved characters, and PID requests
# sometimes arrive as `01 0 \r` / `NO DATA`. That is reported here as raw evidence only.
#
